WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:02.389
INTRO:
By now, I’m sure you’ve all heard about

00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.100
the new iPhone 14.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.920
It’s basically identical to the iPhone 13
even including the internal chip.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.269
One thing that Apple should get credit for
is keeping the prices the same atleast for

00:00:14.269 --> 00:00:17.820
American customers despite the record high
inflation.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.999
The same, however, cannot be said about European
customers who experienced price hikes across

00:00:22.999 --> 00:00:24.220
the board.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:26.670
Take the iPhone 14 Pro Max for example.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:31.279
In the US, this phone comes in at $1,100.

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:38.859
In the UK, this phone comes in at £1,199
and in Germany, this phone comes in at €1,449.

00:00:38.859 --> 00:00:45.989
This translates to $1,385 in the UK and an
eyewatering $1,450 in Germany.

00:00:45.989 --> 00:00:49.570
It wasn’t just the pro series that saw these
price bumps either.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:54.230
All the standard iPhone models went up by
£70 or €100.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:58.280
This means that the average European will
need to spend 30% more than an American to

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.989
get the same product.

00:00:59.989 --> 00:01:03.399
Most people have written this off as pure
greed from Apple.

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:07.400
If Apple had risen prices in the US, the media
would’ve shredded them.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.320
But, by only increasing prices in Europe,
they’ve been able to substantially reduce

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.500
the backlash.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.080
This argument definitely has a lot of merit
to it given that Apple didn’t exactly need

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.540
to raise prices given their healthy profit
margins.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.920
But, with that being said, there is also a
legitimate reason for raising prices only

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:25.920
in Europe.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.300
To put it bluntly, European currencies are
currently performing a cliff dive maneuver

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.130
in comparison to the US dollar.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:37.071
The conversion rate from Euros to Dollars
is at 20 year lows, and this isn’t some

00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:38.530
new phenomenon either.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:44.061
In fact, the Euro has been consistently losing
dominance to the dollar since mid 2008 when

00:01:44.061 --> 00:01:46.970
you could get $1.60 using just €1.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.310
Today, however, you can barely get a single
dollar with a single Euro.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.420
A similar story can be seen with the pound
as well.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.390
The pound is currently at a 37 year low against
the dollar and it has been on a consistent

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.210
decline since late 2007.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:06.980
At the peak, you could get over $2 with a
single pound, but today, you can only get

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:09.700
$1.15 with a single pound.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.269
When you consider these shifts in currency
values, Apple’s price hikes while still

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:16.079
insane, make a little bit more sense.

00:02:16.079 --> 00:02:23.230
But, this brings up the question, why in the
world are European currencies doing so bad?

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:30.319
DEAD WEIGHT:
Taking a look back, this is clearly not a

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:31.319
new trend.

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:35.410
This downtrend has been forming for well over
a decade and the recent issues have simply

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.109
worsened the situation.

00:02:37.109 --> 00:02:39.730
So, what set off this chain of events?

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.079
Well, one of the biggest fundamental issues
plaguing the Euro since its creation is the

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:47.690
fact that it’s not just representative of
one country.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:50.200
Initially, this was seen as a benefit.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.260
The Euro promised to unify the Eurozone countries
economically and make trade between these

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.590
neighboring countries easier than ever before.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:01.940
This unification also promised to make Europe
as a whole a stronger economic force against

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:02.940
the US.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:08.150
But, the problem with a unified currency is
that its virtually impossible to set economic

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.650
policies that are appropriate for every member.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:15.139
You see, with America, while the various states
have differences culturally and politically,

00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:18.120
they’re extremely homogenous economically.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.190
When Californian businesses are suffering
from a bear market, so are Texan businesses.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:26.819
Similarly, when New York businesses are booming,
it’s more than likely that Floridian businesses

00:03:26.819 --> 00:03:28.409
are also booming.

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:32.819
So, it makes sense for all these states to
use the same currency and for the Fed to set

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:34.260
overarching economic policies.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.409
The same, however, cannot be said about the
members of the Eurozone, and especially the

00:03:39.409 --> 00:03:41.439
poorly performing members.

00:03:41.439 --> 00:03:45.189
Over the past 15 years, this has primarily
been Spain and Greece.

00:03:45.189 --> 00:03:49.790
You see, Greece basically got wiped out by
the 2008 financial crisis.

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.310
If the EU didn’t bail them out, Greece would’ve
defaulted on their debt and spiraled into

00:03:54.310 --> 00:03:55.629
a depression.

00:03:55.629 --> 00:04:00.870
During the worst of it, Greece’s budget
deficit was equivalent to 12.9% of their GDP.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:06.129
For perspective, that would be equivalent
to the US have a budget deficient of $2.7

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:07.510
trillion per year.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:11.470
At first, Greece tried to play it cool and
assured everyone that they would get their

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.690
debt in check within 2 years.

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:18.870
But, just 4 months later, Greece would admit
that could default at any moment.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.200
Around the same time, Spain was going through
their own economic crisis.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.540
Like Greece, Spain’s economy was devastated
by the 2008 financial crisis and they were

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.360
spiraling into a depression.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:35.210
As an outside observer, Americans could feel
bad these countries and their people, but

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.640
economically speaking they weren’t all that
affected.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.390
The same, however, could not be said about
Eurozone countries who had skin in the game.

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:48.220
Greece and Spain were already massive deadweights
bringing down the Euro, but if these countries

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.590
collapsed, oh boy, it would be bad.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:56.460
So, the prosperous Eurozone countries had
no choice but to bail out these failing economies.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.990
The EU and the IMF would provide 240 billion
euros worth of emergency funds to greece and

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.260
a 100 billion euro rescue package to Spain.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.970
And where do you think all this money came
from?

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:12.600
It of course came from quantitative easing
also known as money printing.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.660
Since 2008, the Euro money supply has literally
doubled.

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.590
To make things worse, the bailouts weren’t
all that impactful either.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:26.350
Sure, they helped these countries stay afloat
and not default, but fundamentally, they didn’t

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:31.420
change anything, and the underlying economies
would just continue to get worse.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:38.020
A BREAKING POINT:
While Spain and Greece were undergoing economic

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:43.710
hell, many EU countries like the UK and Germany
were actually recovering extraordinarily well.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:48.040
They’re unemployment graphs were plummeting
and their need for money printing was dwindling.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.750
However, they couldn’t stop the money printing
because certain Eurozone countries like Spain

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.170
and Greece were still very much feeling the
pain.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:06:00.770
In both Spain and Greece, unemployment woud
rocket to 25 to 30%.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:06.450
For perspective, unemployment didn’t cross
25% even during the worst of the great depression.

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:11.040
So, these countries were no longer just experiencing
a financial crisis.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.200
They were in a full fledged economic depression
and this was painfully evident in their GDP

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.200
numbers.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:22.950
Spain’s GDP would fall from a peak of $1.6
trillion to just under $1.2 trillion.

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:29.180
Similarly, Greece’s GDP would fall from
a peak of $354 billion to $192 billion.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:34.960
Even today, neither of these countries have
been able to return to their peak GDPs.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:40.280
And this becomes even worse when you consider
that Europe has been experiencing record inflation

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.610
meaning that their inflation adjusted GDPs
are even worse.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:48.220
Also, while I’ve mainly been picking on
Greece and Spain throughout this video, these

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.080
guys were by no means the only perpetrators,
though they were the worst.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.820
Some of the other countries dragging down
the Euro included Cyprus, Portugal, Italy,

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.080
Slovakia, and even France.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.030
And this left the ECB in an extremely difficult
situation.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.810
What were they supposed to do?

00:07:05.810 --> 00:07:10.330
On one side, they have countries like the
UK, Germany, and Denmark were unemployment

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.720
was reaching historically low levels.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:17.090
These countries indicated that it was time
to chill out on the money printing and potentially

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:19.190
even start quantitative tightening.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:23.560
However, on the other end of the spectrum,
you had the perpetrators who were in a more

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.880
dire state than ever.

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:30.850
These countries indicated that it might even
be time to pump up the money printing.

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:34.620
The ECB would end up siding with the countries
that needed help and they would keep economic

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:35.810
policies loose.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:40.710
While this was probably a godsend for the
struggling countries, the prosperous countries

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:44.520
weren’t exactly happy and rightfully so.

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:49.520
The prosperous countries didn’t really benefit
all that much from the loose economic policies,

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:52.990
but they were very much harmed by its accompanying
side effects.

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:56.150
They were starting to feel that they brought
significantly more to the table than they

00:07:56.150 --> 00:08:00.610
were receiving, and this feeling is what would
eventually evolve into Brexit.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:06.810
In June of 2016, Britain would vote on leaving
the EU, and after years of negotiations and

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:11.650
discussions, Brexit would finally take place
on January 31, 2020.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:15.240
Much of England felt like they had finally
gotten a fresh start and that they were no

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.250
longer weighed down by the EU.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:22.300
But, little did they know, the pandemic was
just around the corner.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:27.620
FUEL TO THE FIRE:
Starting off with the most obvious economic

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:30.930
side effect of the pandemic, we have the money
printing.

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:35.349
People often like to criticize the US for
their excessive money printing as they should,

00:08:35.349 --> 00:08:39.899
but what people often forget is that the whole
world has been printing just as much money

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:41.789
if not even more.

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:45.569
Take the UK for example who has printed £895
billion.

00:08:45.569 --> 00:08:52.190
At the average conversion rate over the past
2 years, this is equivalent to $1.14 trillion.

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:59.079
Now, the US has grown its money supply by
a far larger amount coming it at roughly $6.2

00:08:59.079 --> 00:09:03.069
trillion, but you have to look at it in relative
terms.

00:09:03.069 --> 00:09:08.240
Compared to the American GDP of $21 trillion,
American printing works out to roughly 30%

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:09.750
of their GDP.

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:16.000
Compared to the English GDP of $2.7 trillion,
however, English printing works out to 42%

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.480
of their GDP.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:23.819
The ECB hasn’t been nearly as aggressive
with their money printing, but they’ve been

00:09:23.819 --> 00:09:27.110
much more lenient when it comes to interest
rates.

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:32.879
The ECB literally maintained negative interest
rates into 2022, and they’re just now starting

00:09:32.879 --> 00:09:34.870
to aggressively hike rates.

00:09:34.870 --> 00:09:39.370
All of that extra relative printing from the
UK and loose economic policies from the ECB

00:09:39.370 --> 00:09:44.509
have to be reflected somewhere, and that somewhere
seems to be the conversion rates.

00:09:44.509 --> 00:09:47.810
Economic policies aren’t the only battle
that Europe has been fighting though.

00:09:47.810 --> 00:09:50.259
They’ve also been hit hard by Russian sanctions.

00:09:50.259 --> 00:09:55.379
You see, Europe has historically been extremely
dependent on Russia for oil and natural gas.

00:09:55.379 --> 00:09:58.860
Now that the west has decided to sanction
off Russia, Europe has been forced to find

00:09:58.860 --> 00:10:02.830
new energy partners which has been anything
but easy.

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:07.540
The problem is that the entire world knows
that Europe is desperate for energy which

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:12.360
means that sellers can charge a premium and
that’s exactly what they’ve been doing.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:18.260
Gas prices across the notable European countries
are well above $5 per gallon if not over $6

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:19.260
per gallon.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:22.920
A similar story can be seen with electricity
prices as well.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:27.300
Meanwhile, Russia is simply adding fuel to
the fire by essentially retaliating against

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:28.360
the sanctions.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.459
You see, Europe has yet to fully cut off Russia.

00:10:31.459 --> 00:10:35.360
They still receive a significant amount of
crude oil through pipelines like the Nord

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:36.360
Stream 1.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.480
This specific pipeline was closed in July
for 10 days for regular maintenance.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.579
But, when the pipeline was reopened, it was
only operating at 20% capacity.

00:10:45.579 --> 00:10:50.160
Russia would close down the pipeline again
at the end of August citing an apparent leak,

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.600
and it has yet to be reopened as of September
8.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:00.589
In reality, it seems like Russia is simply
halting oil flow on purpose to spike energy

00:11:00.589 --> 00:11:02.319
prices in Europe.

00:11:02.319 --> 00:11:07.230
As Europeans are forced to spend more on energy,
they’re naturally gonna pull back on discretionary

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:08.230
spending.

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:12.290
And given that the central banks are also
starting to pull back, this sets up the perfect

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:14.870
storm for an economic disaster.

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:21.750
THE STATE OF EUROPE:
At the end of the day, the Euro was created

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.450
with good intentions.

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:27.459
It aimed to unify Europe economically and
create a global powerhouse.

00:11:27.459 --> 00:11:33.189
However, a major shortfall of the system is
the vast inequality between the member countries.

00:11:33.189 --> 00:11:37.230
This has forced the prosperous countries to
constantly prop up the failing countries which

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:40.060
hasn’t been particularly helpful for either
of them.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:44.899
The UK has decided to take this issue into
their own hands and become the first country

00:11:44.899 --> 00:11:49.380
to leave the EU, but this hasn’t worked
out all that well either.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:54.249
The pandemic along with the russian sanctions
have taken a massive toll on all European

00:11:54.249 --> 00:12:00.779
countries, and most of them are simply fighting
to put off a nasty recession bring down inflation.

00:12:00.779 --> 00:12:04.269
Compared to this, the US is actually doing
really well.

00:12:04.269 --> 00:12:08.459
We’ve been ahead of the curve when it comes
to economic tightening and the recession isn’t

00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:10.420
that bad, atleast not yet.

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:15.980
So, naturally, the US dollar has been destroying
European currencies, and by the looks of it,

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.940
that’s not gonna to be stopping anytime
soon.

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:23.980
So, Europe better get used to those iPhone
price hikes because they’re likely permanent,

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.939
but that’s just what I think.

00:12:25.939 --> 00:12:28.290
What do you think about European iPhone prices?

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:29.680
Comment that down below.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:33.589
Also, drop a like if you hope Europe can turn
things around.

